## Language

This project's own artifacts — this file, `SETUP_CHECKLIST.md`, `STATUS.md`, task files, scope docs, session logs — are written in **{{PROJECT_LANGUAGE}}**, set during `setup` by asking, never inferred from whatever language a session happens to be held in. Spanish here means Mexican Spanish with full orthography: every accent and diacritic, never stripped.

**The kit stays in English regardless** — skills, scripts, templates, and the output they print. It is shared infrastructure, and one language is what keeps it readable and reviewable. Tool output in English next to project documents in another language is the accepted trade. The one exception is the control center page (`ae-kit-templates/control-center.html`), which carries its texts in English and Spanish and whose language is asked per project the first time it is created.

## Maturity

`maturity:` lives in `STATUS.md` and is changed only by {{OWNER_NAME}}. It governs how much sandbox and eval rigor applies. Never infer or change it.

## Skills

`open-session` opens every session; `session-log` closes it. A session opens by asking {{OWNER_NAME}} what to do, except for tasks they authorized in `STATUS.md` to start without asking (R32). In between, the default sequence for any non-trivial task:

`scope → design → adversarial → build → verify → commit → code-review` **once per unit of work**, then `ship` once

`scope` splits the task into units, one per commit. Everything from `build` onward runs once per unit: build it, verify it, commit it locally, review that commit. `code-review` comes after `commit` because the commit is what gets reviewed, so it has to exist first — **the gate is the push, not the commit.** `ship` runs once per task, when every unit has passed its review with no open blocking findings: it closes the task, pushes, and verifies the remote. Nothing unreviewed reaches the remote, and the whole task goes out in one push at the end.

`adversarial` runs **before** `build`, not after: it produces the list of cases the feature must survive, in plain language, reviewed for gaps by an independent subagent, and {{OWNER_NAME}} receives only the list's class A decisions (the whole list, if that review could not run) — and `build` then implements against that list instead of discovering the unhappy paths in production. Skip it only for a pure copy change or a one-line fix.

`verify` branches: `verify-tests` (always), `verify-evals` (only for non-deterministic model output), `verify-ui` (only if a UI flow is touched), `verify-rls` (only if a table or access policy changes). Each skill's `SKILL.md` states its own trigger, procedure, and — in an `output:` block — the artifact it must produce. **The next skill in a chain checks that artifact exists before it starts.** A skill that cannot name its output as a locatable artifact is under-designed.

